Work Completed:
Developed Section 32 of the MW–M31 merger analysis pipeline, starting from a Claude generated scaffold, then introducing an autocorrelation-based framework for orbital frequency analysis as an alternative to conventional Fourier methods. Implemented FFT-accelerated autocorrelation routines to estimate dominant orbital periods and frequencies directly from particle trajectories, including normalization, peak detection, and robust handling of incomplete time series. Extended the methodology to sliding-window analyses to track the temporal evolution of radial, azimuthal, and vertical orbital frequencies throughout the merger.

Designed and implemented correlation-based diagnostics for orbital regularity by quantifying the persistence of temporal memory through autocorrelation decay. Developed a correlation complexity metric to distinguish regular and chaotic trajectories, together with statistical measures of orbital period stability including standard deviation, drift rate, and coefficient of variation. Integrated resonance identification routines by comparing measured orbital period ratios against low-order rational resonances, enabling automated classification of resonant orbital populations and temporal tracking of resonance fractions during the simulation.

Produced a comprehensive visualization and reporting suite including representative autocorrelation functions, orbital period distributions, temporal heatmaps, resonance statistics, correlation complexity diagnostics, population summary panels, and a three-panel animation illustrating the evolution of orbital periods, resonant fractions, and correlation complexity. Refined data structures, pre-allocation strategies, output generation, and documentation to maintain consistency with the broader MW–M31 analysis pipeline while providing an independent correlation-based framework for validating frequency-space diagnostics.
